==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm}} {{term|loitren|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|dum}} {{term|loteren||to shake, wag, wobble|lang=dum}}, ultimately connected with a frequentative form of {{etyl|gem-pro}} {{recons|lūtaną||to bend, stoop, cower, shrink from, decline|lang=gem-pro}}, see {{l|en|lout}}. Cognate with Modern Dutch {{term|leuteren||to dawdle|lang=nl}}, Swiss German {{term|lottern||to wobble|lang=gsw}}, German {{term|Lotterbube||rascal|lang=de}}. More at {{l|en|lout}}, {{l|en|little}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈlɔɪtə/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|ˈlɔjɾɚ}}
* {{audio|en-us-loiter.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|ɔɪtə(r)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To stand about without any aim or purpose; to stand about [[idly]]; to [[linger]]; to [[hang around]].
#: ''For some reason, they discourage '''loitering''' outside the store, but encourage it inside.''

